Here is a simple C++ program to find the nth Fibonacci number using recursion:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;

// Function definition
int fibonacci_recursive(int n);

int main() {
    // Print Fibonacci series
    for (int n = 0; n < 10; n++) {
        cout << "\nFirst " << n << " Fibonacci numbers: ";
        cout << fibonacci_recursive(n) << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Recursive function definition
int fibonacci_recursive(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) return n;
    else return fibonacci_recursive(n - 1) + fibonacci_recursive(n - 2);
}
```

This program defines a function `fibonacci_recursive` which takes an input parameter `n` and returns the nth Fibonacci number. The function uses recursion to calculate the nth Fibonacci number by using the previous two numbers from the series to calculate the current number. It first checks if the input parameter is 0 or 1, which are the first two numbers in the Fibonacci series. If not, it then calculates the difference between n-1 and n-2 and adds the result to the current Fibonacci number. Finally, it returns the result.</s>

